WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.144
The leading prior method, Checkmate,

00:00:04.224 --> 00:00:08.369
casts rematerialization as a mixed-integer linear program.

00:00:08.449 --> 00:00:12.593
It is expressive,

00:00:12.673 --> 00:00:16.818
but its number of Boolean decision variables grows
with the square of the number of nodes in the graph.

00:00:16.898 --> 00:00:21.042
That quadratic growth becomes a wall: for graphs
with a few hundred nodes and a few thousand edges,

00:00:21.122 --> 00:00:25.267
Checkmate either times out or runs out of memory
during the solve.

00:00:25.347 --> 00:00:29.491
Because the trend toward on-device training and
stricter latency targets keeps enlarging these graphs,

00:00:29.571 --> 00:00:33.716
we need a formulation whose complexity grows much
more slowly,

00:00:33.796 --> 00:00:37.940
so realistic graphs can be solved within an
acceptable compile time.
